 found beyond the artifacts measured\\ndirectly above (searched: `docs/`, package roots, `README.md` files \u2014 none contain benchmark data).\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 drift-audit methodology precedents (two, at different rigor levels)\\n\\n**Precedent 1 (lighter, package-scoped): `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md`.**\\nMethod, reconstructed from the file itself: pick one spec doc + its implementing modules; use\\n`git log`/blame on both to build a timeline correlating which commit touched code vs. doc; walk\\nthe implementation and classify each finding into one of 4 *drift-type* categories (Implemented\\nbut Undocumented / Documented but Unimplemented / Outdated Documentation / Mismatched Details);\\neach finding records: doc location + claim, code location + reality, and a recommendation;\\nfindings are then triaged into a flat Critical/High/Medium/Low priority list. Sample finding\\n(FIND-1) confirms the per-entry shape: doc claim (with line ref), code reality (with line ref and\\nsnippet), single recommendation \u2014 no adjudication field distinguishing \\\"doc wrong\\\" from \\\"code\\nwrong\\\" from \\\"neither is right.\\\" This is a genuine, working precedent, but it categorizes *what kind*\\nof drift occurred, not *which side should change* \u2014 item #3054's Deliverable 4 explicitly requires\\nthe latter (three adjudication outcomes: doc correct/impl must change; impl correct/doc must\\nchange; neither correct/new direction supplied). Precedent 1's shape needs an added adjudication\\naxis to satisfy Deliverable 4 as written, not to be discarded.\\n\\n**Precedent 2 (heavier, plugin-wide, closer fit): `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` three-file\\npipeline** (`purpose-audit-reference-manifest.yaml`, `purpose-audit-clause-evidence-manifest.yaml`,\\n`purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`), auditing `docs/PURPOSE.md` against the whole plugin at\\nbaseline commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`, all marked `frozen: true`. Structure:\\n\\n1. **Reference-selection manifest**: declares a `source_precedence` order (target contract clauses\\n   \u2192 current-boundary clauses \u2192 executable protocols/config/transport/provider implementations \u2192\\n   published plugin/lifecycle/skill contracts \u2192 focused tests \u2192 supporting ADRs/procedures), an\\n   explicit include/exclude selection rule, and an `audit_safety` block (`mode: read-only`,\\n   isolated backend defaults) \u2014 this fixes what counts as evidence and how the audit avoids\\n   mutating real state before any finding is produced.\\n2. **Clause/evidence manifest**: atomically maps every clause of the target doc to selected\\n   sources, with an explicit `evidence_schema` classifying each assertion as `documented-claim`\\n   (what the system declares) / `implementation-fact` (a structural property observable in\\n   committed source) / `test-evidence` (an executable test's assertion) / `confidence`. This is\\n   directly a per-claim fact-check method matching Deliverable 4's per-discrepancy record\\n   requirement.\\n3. **Remediation plan**: dependency-ordered tasks derived from the partitioned (A-D) audit\\n   findings, with a `decision_log` of resolved user decisions (each entry: id, date, clause_ids,\\n   origin) that closes a \\\"needs user decision\\\" flag raised by a partition auditor \u2014 this is\\n   functionally the three-outcome adjudication mechanism Deliverable 4 asks for, already\\n   implemented and used at plugin scale (not package scale) in this repo.\\n\\nPrecedent 2 is the closer methodological fit for #3054's Deliverable 4 scope (whole plugin,\\nadjudicated outcomes) than Precedent 1 (package-scoped, drift-type-only). A related process audit,\\n`docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` (2026-03-02, method: `/improve-processes`\\nexcellence checklist + triage protocol), is a third precedent but targets workflow-process quality\\nagainst a checklist rubric, not document-vs-code fact-checking \u2014 relevant to the \\\"workflow\\\" leg of\\nDeliverable 4's \\\"code, workflow, and prose instruction alike\\\" but not a drift-audit method on its\\nown.\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility \u2014 Gaps (tool unavailability)\\n\\nNo `WebSearch`, `Ref`, or `context7` MCP tools were present in this agent's available toolset for\\nthis task, despite being named in the loaded api-state/ecosystem-research skill instructions. Per\\nthis repo's own operating expectation (\\\"technology may name an internal module... external angles\\nwill report short Gaps sections, which is expected\\\"), no external OSS convention survey (e.g. how\\nother multi-plugin/monorepo agent-tooling projects structure per-module docs) was performed. The\\nonly external input available \u2014 the installed `mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling` skill's own\\nSKILL.md \u2014 was read directly and is cited in full above; treat it as the entire \\\"ecosystem\\\" input\\nto this note, not a survey.\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\n[CONFLICT] RT-ICA condition 3 on this item's own body states: \\\"CONTEXT.md + root CONTEXT-MAP.md,\\nadded this session.\\\" Verified state at research time: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` is absent from `main` and\\nexists only committed on open PR #3080's unmerged branch (`docs/markdown-engine-decisions`, commit\\n`9bcc2bbf0`); it is placed at the monorepo root, not the plugin root \u2014 a distinction the RT-ICA\\nsnapshot's phrasing does not disambiguate.\\n[RESOLVED \u2014 this is a single verifiable fact, not a disagreement between independent research\\nangles]: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` is committed on PR #3080's branch only (absent from `main`), at path\\n`/CONTEXT-MAP.md` (repo root), containing exactly one context entry pointing to\\n`./plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`. This is itself a live instance of the documentation\\ndrift #3054 targets \u2014 RT-ICA condition 3's wording should be corrected at the next grooming pass\\nto state the file is unmerged (on PR #3080) and mis-rooted, not \\\"added this session\\\" without\\nqualification.\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\nNone applicable \u2014 this was a single-agent pass with no independent second angle to cross-check\\nagainst (tool unavailability, see Ecosystem section above).\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- No external convention survey beyond the one locally-installed skill (`WebSearch`/`Ref`/\\n  `context7` unavailable in this session's toolset).\\n- Runtime/dynamic drift (behaviour only observable by executing code \u2014 e.g. actual FastMCP tool\\n  response shapes vs. documented schemas) was not measured; this note is static-analysis only,\\n  consistent with the codebase-auditor angle's operating constraints.\\n- The 244 `.md` files in the plugin were not individually checked for drift status \u2014 that\\n  execution is Deliverable 4 itself, out of scope for this research pass, which targets RT-ICA\\n  condition 4 (shared root path / naming convention) and drift-audit methodology only.\\n- The `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` pipeline's completion/adoption status beyond its frozen\\n  baseline headers (dated 2026-07-26, commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`) was not traced further \u2014\\n  worth a direct read of `purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`'s full `decision_log` and task list\\n  before Deliverable 4 execution begins, to confirm whether its remediation tasks were carried out\\n  and whether its manifest schema is reusable as-is or needs adaptation for a code-vs-doc (not\\n  purpose-doc-vs-code) audit target.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T06:16:19.324618Z</sub>\\n\\n**Assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n**Alignment Statement**:\\nItem #3054 directly supports the development-harness mission of providing an \\\"agent-facing workflow system expressed in Markdown\\\" where agents reliably move work from intake to validated closure. The component architecture map and documentation-drift audit address a foundational gap in the system's ability to fulfill that mission: ensuring accurate, discoverable documentation of the system's own structure so agents starting work can correctly understand the system they are operating within.\\n\\n**Mission Grounding** (from PURPOSE.md):\\n- \\\"Development Harness targets a generic agent work-management system that preserves logical work and the evidence needed to move it from intake to validated closure\\\"\\n- \\\"The plugin is primarily an agent-facing workflow system expressed in Markdown\\\"\\n\\nThe problem described in the item \u2014 agents misreading single packages as system behaviour, missing existing capabilities, conflating distinct concerns, and working against contradictory documented behaviour \u2014 is a direct threat to this mission. The proposed pass (per-module architecture, concept consolidation, drift audit, cross-reference table) directly enables agents to preserve logical understanding of the system.\\n\\n**Design Principles Grounding** (from AGENTS.md, \\\"Design Principles\\\" section):\\n- \\\"The harness owns the *process*; language plugins own the *specialists*\\\"\\n- \\\"Every stage produces a logical handoff. Document artifacts use `artifact_register` and `artifact_read`; plans and task state use `sam_plan` and `sam_task`. Neither surface exposes direct filesystem paths.\\\"\\n- \\\"Task complexity is context-fit under uncertainty\\\"\\n\\nWithout a component map and authoritative documentation of relationships, the harness cannot deliver on \\\"Every stage produces a logical handoff\\\" \u2014 agents lack the documented interface to understand what each package owns and what stages consume. The drift audit prevents the \\\"context-fit complexity\\\" principle from degrading due to contradictory or stale documentation.\\n\\n**Sequencing Alignment**:\\nThe item correctly notes it is \\\"deliberately sequenced after the targeted markdown-consumption architecture work\\\" \u2014 this adheres to the design principle that logical handoffs must build on settled foundations. The prior work (markdown-consumption contract) establishes the surface; this work documents the internal structure that consumes that surface.\\n\\n**No Conflicts**: No contradiction with stated design principles or existing direction signals in merged PRs. The item represents a systematic correctness fix for agent-facing documentation, not a process change or new capability.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Every code package in the plugin has an architecture description covering role, owns, consumes, consumed-by, and detail location\\n- [ ] Near-collisions between modules and concepts carry explicit distinction notes\\n- [ ] Concepts and glossary consolidated to one definition each, with other references linking rather than restating\\n- [ ] Shared root path and naming convention defined and documented\\n- [ ] Drift audit complete: every plugin document fact-checked against code, workflow, and prose behaviour\\n- [ ] Drift review chart produced, supporting all three adjudication outcomes per entry, and reviewed\\n- [ ] Cross-reference table produced identifying every multi-document topic and its duplicate sources\\n- [ ] The resulting material is reachable from the plugin's discovery index, not orphaned\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs **an agent or contributor working anywhere in the development-harness plugin**, I want **an authoritative per-module architecture description, a consolidated concept glossary, and a verified record of where documentation and behaviour disagree** so that **I can find the right component and the right detail without inferring the system's shape from whichever file I happened to open first**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation, 2026-08-20 \u2014 architecture drift investigation across the backlog and markdown-handling subsystems\\n- **Priority**: P1 \u2014 documentation drift is actively producing incorrect agent work, with repeated instances observed in a single session\\n- **Sequencing**: Deferred. Begins after the targeted markdown-consumption architecture description is settled; that smaller surface serves as the pattern for this broader pass.\\n- **Related**: #3050 (ADR identifiers referenced but undefined \u2014 a subset of the same problem); #3052 (tool documentation omits implemented parameters); PR #3053 (targeted integration of three overlapping documents, done reactively rather than systematically)\\n\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Architecture Baseline\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIntended behaviour and component ownership are recorded in two documents:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` \u2014 normative requirements R1\u2013R8 and the required flow\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` \u2014 package map, ownership boundaries, and do-not-confuse-with notes\\n\\nBoth are under review in PR #3053 (integration and deduplication against existing docs) and PR #3056 (component map, contract scope binding, review-round amendments).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Sequencing\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n#3057 requires the grooming validation gate to read section presence from the engine before the hand-built structures it currently depends on are removed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Gap Items Bridging Current State To The Architecture\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Item | Gap |\\n|---|---|\\n| #3057 | Default item-read path hand-builds its index and pagination instead of using the engine |\\n| #3058 | Plan, task, and artifact reads are unbounded against the harness response cap |\\n| #3059 | Navigation and pagination are reimplemented on top of the engine rather than used from it |\\n| #3060 | Runtime hints steer agents toward full-content retrieval |\\n| #3061 | Sections and artifacts are separate lookups rather than one inventory |\\n| #3062 | Paginated content has no content identity, so pages re-collect and can span versions |\\n| #3063 | The CLI cannot reach address-based navigation |\\n| #3064 | Agent instructions mandate full-item fetches the contract supersedes |\\n\\nRelated, filed separately: #3055 (registered artifacts whose content cannot be read) blocks #3061; #3050 (referenced ADRs with no definition documents); #2453 (agents hardcode section names with no route to a source of truth).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Required Deliverables\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### 1. Per-module architecture and relationships\\n\\nFor every code package in the plugin, a description covering: the module's role in one sentence; what it owns; what it consumes and from where; what consumes it; and where its authoritative detail lives.\\n\\nEach near-collision between modules or concepts carries an explicit distinction note stating that the two are not the same and how to tell them apart. The observed failure mode is not \\\"could not find it\\\" but \\\"found something adjacent and assumed it was the thing\\\" \u2014 the distinction notes exist to stop that specifically.\\n\\n### 2. Authoritative concept consolidation and glossary\\n\\nOne definition per concept, stated once, in a known location. Every other document referencing that concept links to the definition rather than restating it.\\n\\n### 3. Structure design at a shared root path\\n\\nA defined home for this material, with the arrangement and naming convention documented, so that a new module's documentation has an obvious correct location and an agent has an obvious correct place to look.\\n\\n### 4. Documentation-versus-behaviour drift audit\\n\\nEvery existing document in the plugin is fact-checked against what the plugin actually does \u2014 code, workflow, and prose instruction alike. For each discrepancy found, record: the document and the specific claim; the actual observed behaviour; and the nature of the difference.\\n\\nThe output is a review chart listing every discrepancy for adjudication. Each entry must support three outcomes:\\n\\n- the documentation is correct and the implementation must change;\\n- the implementation is correct and the documentation must change;\\n- neither matches the desired architecture, and a written direction is supplied for what the correct state is.\\n\\nThe chart is produced for review. Entries are not resolved unilaterally.\\n\\n### 5. Cross-reference and duplicate-source table\\n\\nA table identifying every topic described in more than one document, showing which documents cover it and where they agree, diverge, or contradict. This is the deduplication work list: each row resolves to one authoritative source with the others reduced to links.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"711d9c68ccbea1c4a8538b35bc8dd03a60f63368f7d65bd2d8abb022e2318fca\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPw5hUw\"}"}